<p class="fine">For planning only. Results depend on the figures you enter and your policy’s terms. They aren’t a quote, an appraisal or legal advice. Example figures are illustrative.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>What the checker shows</h2>
<p>Hailstones gain energy fast as they grow: a 2-inch stone carries roughly seventeen times the impact energy of a 1-inch stone. The checker uses approximate published reference values for ice spheres falling at terminal speed and compares the stone size you pick with damage thresholds commonly cited in roofing research for each covering.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Impact energy and fall speed</strong> are approximations for a solid sphere of ice in still air. Real stones vary in shape and density.</li>
<li><strong>Thresholds</strong> are the approximate hail size at which functional damage to a new covering is often reported. Older or brittle materials can be damaged by smaller hail.</li>
<li><strong>Wind</strong> adds horizontal speed and changes the angle of impact, which is why elevations facing the storm often take the worst damage.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p class="callout-t">Reference only</p><p>Values are illustrative and rounded. They don’t replace an on-site inspection, and a “low” result doesn’t mean no damage occurred — check gutters, vents, screens, siding and window wraps, which dent at smaller sizes.</p></div>
<h2>Using it in a claim</h2>
<p>Hail size is often disputed. Save photos of stones beside a coin or ruler, note the time of the storm, and look up local storm reports. If the carrier says hail was “too small” for your roof, the comparison here is a starting point for a conversation — not the answer.</p>
<h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How do I know what size hail hit my house?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Photos of stones next to a coin or ruler taken during or right after the storm, dent sizes on soft metals like gutters and vents, and local storm reports all help.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are Class 4 shingles hail-proof?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. Class 4 is a laboratory impact rating. Impact-resistant shingles can still be damaged by large hail, and some policies treat cosmetic damage differently.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why does roof age change the result?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Asphalt shingles lose flexibility as they age under strong sun, so older roofs can be damaged by hail below the thresholds cited for new material.</p></div></details></div>
